  - supersession
  - contradiction detection
  - provenance
  - protocol
abstract: >-
  Datum is a versioned canon layer for multi-agent systems: a protocol for
  recording what is true, since when, and on whose authority. Existing agent
  primitives are append-or-retrieve and cannot detect that two things are both
  asserted and cannot both be true. Datum adds authority tiers, declarative
  constraints, and supersession — the primitive that distinguishes a deliberate
  change to canon from an error, which is a retcon in narrative, an amendment in
  policy, and a superseded decision record in engineering. Agents may read,
  check, and propose; only an authorized principal may commit. The repository
  includes the protocol specification, a threat model, the GREATGAME benchmark
  specification for measuring supersession discrimination, and a conformance
  suite. The specification is itself versioned as Datum canon.
references:
  - type: data
    title: "GREATGAME: A Canon-Consistency Benchmark for Automated Contradiction and Supersession Detection"
